architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "0003"
  title: Preflight text scan for forbidden constructs
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-07-11"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: [[[
    SYON must reject a set of YAML constructs: tags, anchors and aliases,
    flow collections, the question-mark complex-key marker, and the
    document markers. In syon-parser, this rejection happens in two
    layers. grammar.pest defines forbidden-construct rules, but per the
    file's own comment they are used in error-reporting; the parser
    rejects them elsewhere. The preflight function in parser.rs does the
    actual rejection: a line-by-line scan of the raw source text, before
    pest parsing runs at all, looking for these constructs outside
    double-quoted strings.

    This split dates back to the saphyr-parser era (ADR 0002): saphyr's
    YAML event stream didn't carry block-vs-flow style, so flow
    collections couldn't be rejected from the event stream and a
    text-level preflight scan was added to cover that gap. When the
    parser was rewritten around pest, the preflight scan was kept rather
    than folded entirely into the grammar.

    syon-go does not have this split -- its forbidden-construct checks
    live inline in the recursive-descent parser itself, checked at the
    point each construct would otherwise be consumed.
    ]]]
  decision: [[[
    Keep a preflight text scan as a distinct pass ahead of grammar-based
    parsing in the Rust implementation, rather than expressing all
    forbidden-construct rejection inside grammar.pest alone.
    ]]]
  consequences:
    positive:
      - Forbidden-construct rejection is expressed once, in plain Rust, without needing every corner of the pest grammar to encode it.
    negative:
      - The preflight scan and the grammar must independently agree on what counts as a value position (e.g. after a key colon or a list dash), and can drift out of sync.
      - This already happened once, a real bug -- the preflight scan's literal-block-delimiter recognition only handled the delimiter alone on its own line, not a delimiter opening a literal block on the same line as its key, even though the grammar and AST layer already supported that form. Every document using that form, including the project's own canonical examples, was rejected as a forbidden flow collection until this was fixed (see the preflight and grammar.pest changes made alongside this ADR log).
      - Any future change to what a value position means needs to be made in both preflight() and grammar.pest, with a test exercising both layers together -- a test on either layer alone would not have caught the bug above.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: Express all forbidden-construct rejection as pest grammar rules, no separate text scan
      reason: Would remove the two-layer drift risk, but is a bigger rewrite than fixing the immediate bug; worth reconsidering as a superseding ADR if this class of bug recurs.
